            <div xml:id="d435">

                <pb facs="0535" n="437" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg437"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/225: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-09T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 9, 1920—5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 9—1:41 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>165. Following summary of telegrams sent to Tientsin.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“July 8, 5 p.m. Probability of military operations between Tuan and Tsao Kun
                        extremely serious. Warn Americans residing in <pb facs="0536" n="438"
                            xml:id="pg438"/> Paotingfu that attack on city by armed forces
                        improbable [<hi rendition="#italic">is probable?</hi>] and for them to be
                        ready to leave at a moments notice, it is preferable for the women and
                        children to leave now.”</p>
                    <p>“July 8, 10 a.m. Attack on Paotingfu from Peking and Maliang from Tsinanfu
                        now in preparation. Advise Americans Paotingfu desirable to leave.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
